# Tenor trombone, slide in first position, one held note: bore, lip, cup, microphone.
# render.sh prepends the schema and audio records and appends the schedule record
# plus a canonical gesture track, so one description serves every held note.
#
# Every number carries its source. UNKNOWN marks what no source opened for this lane
# gives; none of it is fitted to a recording.
#
# --- ambient -------------------------------------------------------------------
# 293.15 K, 101325 Pa, dry: a standard laboratory state. UNKNOWN: the temperature and
# humidity of the Iowa anechoic chamber (MIS.html does not state them) and the bore gas
# temperature while played (breath warms and humidifies the air column).
full_scale_pa 50
ambient moist_air 293.15 101325 0
#
# --- lip ------------------------------------------------------------------------
# J. S. Cullen, "A Study of Brass Instrument Acoustics using an Artificial Lip Reed
# Mechanism, Laser Doppler Anemometry and Other Techniques", PhD thesis, University of
# Edinburgh, 2000, Table A.2 p.253, embouchure 7 (standard font = measured, not
# interpolated): H0 = 0.53 mm, b = 12 mm, fL1 = 204.5 Hz, QL1 = 7.0, 1/muL1 = 0.063
# m^2/kg. The island is the outward-striking mode (L1). These are ARTIFICIAL latex lips
# on a trombone-size mouthpiece, not a player: human outward resonances are lower and
# damped harder (Newton 2009 thesis Table 4.2: 32-60 Hz, Q 1.5-2.4, video method) but
# no human mass, width or rest gap is published. UNKNOWN: the inward-striking mode L2
# (fL2 244.5 Hz, Q 11.0, 1/muL2 -0.073 m^2/kg in the same row) is not represented by a
# 1-DOF island. The face area only normalizes force; m = A/(1/mu) and the motion
# does not depend on it. Table 3.1 of the same thesis gives 1/muL1 = 0.11 for the
# 'medium' embouchure; Table A.2 is the later, systematically lower set (thesis p.63).
lip_response 204.5 7.0 0.063 0.012 0.00053 1.0e-4
lip_source Cullen (2000) PhD Edinburgh Table A.2 p.253 embouchure 7: fL1 204.5 Hz, QL1 7.0, 1/muL1 0.063 m2/kg, b 12 mm, H0 0.53 mm; artificial lips, outward-striking mode only; face area 1e-4 m2 is a normalization that cancels
#
# --- mouthpiece cup ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The cup and throat are GEOMETRY in the bore below (element 1, digitized from Braden's Fig 5.3: rim radius
# 1.286 cm falling to a 0.39 cm throat within 3.8 cm, about 9.5 ml for the first 3.5 cm). A lumped cup
# volume would count it twice, so the lumped volume is the smallest value the file format admits (1 mm^3,
# under 0.01 percent of the cup); it is a format floor, not a measurement. The first native renders used
# Cullen's rig mouthpiece volume (12.7 ml, thesis p.41) here AND a placeholder cylinder for element 1: that
# double counted the cup, and moving the volume alone moved the emergent pitches by 12 to 50 cents.
cup_volume_m3 1e-9
termination flanged
modes 3 1
# Mouth-flow line ladder. The first native render (6f2e42ec7) was REFUSED by the owner's default ladder
# (relative tail energy 1e-10): at the 65536-tap cap (3.4 s on this clock) the last quarter of the
# mouth-flow impulse response still held 1.9e-8 of its energy. With this tolerance declared the ladder
# stops at 8192 taps and the sidecar records every rung: tail energy 4.4e-7 (2048 taps), 2.4e-7 (4096),
# 1.4e-7 (8192). The tail HALVES per doubling, an algebraic 1/N decay, which is the signature of an
# endpoint discontinuity of the sampled response (the 20 Hz stand-in for the DC bin, or the real Nyquist
# bin), NOT of a long physical ring-down: a physical ring would fall exponentially. UNVERIFIED (no code
# was run to test it). Because the receiver differentiates the flow, a DC-side step is suppressed in the
# radiated pressure. Declared instead of hidden: 1e-6 relative energy is about -60 dB of wrap-around
# amplitude, far below the tens of dB the grading measures resolve. The default 1e-10 stays the default
# for every other file, and the default can never be met by doubling while the tail falls only as 1/N.
mouth_flow_tail_tol 1e-6
#
# --- bore -------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A. C. P. Braden, "Bore Optimisation and Impedance Modelling of Brass Musical
# Instruments", PhD thesis, University of Edinburgh, 2006, Table 5.1 p.72 "Templated
# description of a trombone (units/cm)": a 14-element template that approximates the
# measured bore of the author's Conn 88H large-bore tenor (p.71 'specific values were
# chosen to approximate this bore profile'; p.110 'The template in table 5.1 was used to
# reconstruct the 88H'). Template values, not raw calipers; the thesis states no
# uncertainty. Radii and lengths are printed in cm, converted to m below. JUMP elements
# (2, 4, 6, 9) are the radius steps between neighbouring records.
# Element 1's interior is digitized from the thesis figure (see below). Specimen caveat: the UIowa tenor trombone model is 'NA' on MIStenortrombone.html, so
# this is a population stand-in (QP verdict label: population-flagged), not the
# recorded instrument. The F attachment is not represented (p.92 only gives '1 m').
#
# Element 1 LIST 'Mouthpiece & lead-pipe', length 26.145 cm, radius 1.286 -> 0.664 cm (printed). The table
# prints only the ends; the interior below is DIGITIZED FROM FIGURE 5.3 (thesis p.71), not a measured table:
# see braden2006-fig5-3-leadpipe.tsv for the method, the axis calibration, the checks against printed values
# and the estimated uncertainty (radius about +-0.015 cm, axial position about +-0.3 cm, cup volume about
# +-1.5 ml). The first and last radii are the printed ones. A radius step at 7.8 cm (backbore 0.516 cm to
# leadpipe 0.617 cm) is drawn by the thesis as a short ramp and is kept as a step. 33 segments.
cone 0.012860 0.012561 0.006188
cone 0.012561 0.012023 0.003868
cone 0.012023 0.011485 0.002321
cone 0.011485 0.010947 0.002321
cone 0.010947 0.010409 0.001547
cone 0.010409 0.008929 0.002321
cone 0.008929 0.008391 0.001547
cone 0.008391 0.007584 0.001547
cone 0.007584 0.006508 0.001547
cone 0.006508 0.005701 0.001547
cone 0.005701 0.004894 0.001934
cone 0.004894 0.004087 0.001934
cone 0.004087 0.003885 0.009282
cone 0.003885 0.004019 0.006188
cone 0.004019 0.004288 0.006188
cone 0.004288 0.004557 0.006188
cone 0.004557 0.004759 0.006188
cone 0.004759 0.004961 0.006188
cone 0.004961 0.005163 0.009282
cone 0.006171 0.006037 0.018564
cone 0.006037 0.005970 0.012376
cone 0.005970 0.006037 0.012376
cone 0.006037 0.006104 0.012376
cone 0.006104 0.006171 0.012376
cone 0.006171 0.006306 0.012376
cylinder 0.006306 0.012376
cylinder 0.006306 0.012376
cone 0.006306 0.006441 0.012376
cylinder 0.006441 0.012376
cylinder 0.006441 0.012376
cylinder 0.006441 0.012376
cone 0.006441 0.006575 0.012376
cone 0.006575 0.006640 0.016244
# Element 3 CYLR 'Main slide' 139.439 cm, r 0.695 cm
cylinder 0.00695 1.39439
# Element 5 CYLR 'Neck-pipe' 26.910 cm, r 0.725 cm
cylinder 0.00725 0.26910
# Element 7 CONE 'Tuning-slide' 21.990 cm, r 0.755 -> 0.990 cm
cone 0.00755 0.00990 0.21990
# Element 8 CYLR 'Sleeve' 6.732 cm, r 0.990 cm
cylinder 0.00990 0.06732
# Element 10 CONE 'Bell' 5.336 cm, r 1.053 -> 1.105 cm
cone 0.01053 0.01105 0.05336
# Elements 11-14 BESS 'Bell': Bessel horns r = b (x0 - s)^-flare (thesis App. B,
# eqs B.1-B.3 and Eq. 5.4), end radii and flare as printed; each expanded into 16 cones
# on the exact curve (refinement axis: the last number).
bessel 0.01105 0.01507 0.15042 1.260 16
bessel 0.01507 0.02228 0.15042 0.894 16
bessel 0.02228 0.04118 0.09685 0.494 16
bessel 0.04118 0.10860 0.05593 1.110 16
bore_source Braden (2006) PhD Edinburgh Table 5.1 p.72, 14-element template of the author's Conn 88H large-bore tenor (cm to m); element 1 (mouthpiece and leadpipe) interior DIGITIZED FROM FIG 5.3 p.71 with estimated uncertainty (braden2006-fig5-3-leadpipe.tsv), not a measured table; UIowa trombone model is NA so this is a population stand-in; no F attachment
#
# --- microphone -------------------------------------------------------------------
# UIowa MIS pages: 'Distance 5 feet' = 1.524 m, anechoic chamber, Neumann KM 84
# cardioid (MIStenortrombone.html). The point is on the bell axis in the baffle frame:
# UNKNOWN: the player's bell-to-microphone orientation and the KM 84 directivity are
# not modelled. 128 x 512 is the receiver's maximum quadrature; for the 0.1086 m bell
# the receiver admits a resolved band of c/(8 cell) = 3.92 kHz, so 3900 Hz is declared.
microphone 0 0 1.524 128 512 3900
